theory.intro:
A sine wave and a cosine wave with the same angle add to a single wave: $a\cos x + b\sin x$ becomes $R\cos(x \pm \alpha)$ or $R\sin(x \pm \alpha)$. Here $R = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2} > 0$ and $\alpha$ is the **auxiliary angle**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.3]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (4,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (4,3);
\draw (O) -- (P) node[midway, below] {$a$};
\draw (P) -- (Q) node[midway, right] {$b$};
\draw (Q) -- (O) node[midway, above left] {$R=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$};
\coordinate (p1) at ($(P)!0.3cm!(O)$);
\coordinate (p2) at ($(P)!0.3cm!(Q)$);
\draw (p1) -- ($(p1)+(p2)-(P)$) -- (p2);
\pic[draw, "$\alpha$", angle radius=0.8cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = P--O--Q};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
